**Goal** — Add theme-owned table glyphs and the pure table model and deterministic column-sizing contract.

**Motivation** — Models answer with tables often (status summaries, option comparisons), so this is the most visible remaining markdown gap.

**Approach** — One deterministic renderer, no new dependency. A new pure module `packages/nax/src/tui/chat/markdown-table.ts` owns column sizing and drawing over already-styled cell spans, so it does not import `markdown.ts` (no import cycle): `markdown.ts` turns each cell's inline tokens into spans with its existing `inlineSpans`, builds a `TableModel`, and calls `renderTable` through its `_markdownDeps` seam.

**Column sizing (`columnWidths`).** The natural width of a column is the widest cell in it (header included), measured in terminal columns with `spansWidth`, and at least 1. The budget is `width - (3n + 1)`. If the natural widths fit the budget they are used. Otherwise each column's floor is `min(natural, MIN_CELL_WIDTH)` with `MIN_CELL_WIDTH = 3`; if the floors do not fit the budget, `columnWidths` returns `undefined` (stack). Otherwise it shrinks one column by one column-width at a time, always the currently widest column above its floor (on a tie, the leftmost), until the widths sum to the budget. Example: natural `[10, 4, 20]` with budget 20 gives `[8, 4, 8]`.

Target: the same fields plus `readonly table: TableGlyphs`, with a new exported interface below. Unicode set: `─ │ ┌ ┬ ┐ ├ ┼ ┤ └ ┴ ┘` (in field order). ASCII set: `horizontal` `-`, `vertical` `|`, every corner and join `+`.

Module rules: `src/tui/chat/` sibling imports are relative (`./wrap`), `markdown-table.ts` is not added to the `src/tui/chat/index.ts` barrel (it has no consumer outside the directory), new source stays under the 600-line gate.

**Scope** — In: extend `theme.ts` and its glyph tests; create `markdown-table.ts` and `markdown-table.test.ts` with model types, options, constant and sizing tests. `columnWidths` consumes supplied natural widths; measuring model cells belongs to US-002. Out: grid drawing and `renderTable` implementation (US-002), stacked drawing/degenerate render width (US-003), markdown token dispatch and layout regression updates (US-004). Changing the fence glyphs (`codeTop`, `codeSide`, `codeBottom`) or sharing them with the table glyph set. A configurable `MIN_CELL_WIDTH`, column-width hints, or a per-table style choice. Adding any package dependency for table rendering; the renderer is built on `marked`'s existing table token and `wrap.ts`. Exporting `markdown-table.ts` from the `src/tui/chat/index.ts` barrel; it has no consumer outside `src/tui/chat/`. `markdown-table.ts` importing `markdown.ts`; cells arrive as styled spans so the dependency runs one way only.

**Interface** — In `theme.ts`:
```ts
export interface TableGlyphs {
  readonly horizontal: string;
  readonly vertical: string;
  readonly topLeft: string;
  readonly topJoin: string;
  readonly topRight: string;
  readonly midLeft: string;
  readonly midJoin: string;
  readonly midRight: string;
  readonly bottomLeft: string;
  readonly bottomJoin: string;
  readonly bottomRight: string;
}
```
Add `readonly table: TableGlyphs` to `ChatGlyphs`, retaining all existing fields. In the new `markdown-table.ts`:
```ts
import type { Span, StyledLine } from "./styled-line";
import type { TableGlyphs } from "./theme";

export type TableAlign = "left" | "center" | "right" | null;

export interface TableModel {
  readonly header: readonly (readonly Span[])[];
  readonly rows: readonly (readonly (readonly Span[])[])[];
  readonly align: readonly TableAlign[];
}

export interface RenderTableOptions {
  readonly width: number;
  readonly glyphs: TableGlyphs;
  /** The rule glyph the stacked layout separates rows with. */
  readonly rule: string;
}

/** The narrowest a column shrinks to before the table stacks. */
export const MIN_CELL_WIDTH = 3;

/** Content width per column for `width`, or undefined when the floors do not fit (stack). */
export function columnWidths(natural: readonly number[], width: number): readonly number[] | undefined;
```
The function declaration is the required exported signature; supply its implementation in this story. The `StyledLine` type is used by the renderer added in US-002.

**Goal** — Complete `renderTable` with stacked output when column floors cannot fit and make degenerate widths behave like width 1.

**Motivation** — Models answer with tables often (status summaries, option comparisons), so this is the most visible remaining markdown gap.

**Approach** — **Stacked layout.** When `columnWidths` returns `undefined`, each body row becomes one block: per column, the bold header spans, an unstyled `": "`, then the cell spans, wrapped together to the width with `wrap` (no hanging indent). A column whose header text is empty draws the cell spans alone. A column whose cell is empty or missing draws the bold header and `:` with no trailing space (`wrap` drops a trailing separator); a column whose header and cell are both empty draws no line. A body row whose every column draws no line produces no block and no rule, so two rules are never adjacent. Blocks are separated by one muted line of the rule glyph repeated to the width. A header-only table (no body rows) stacks as one bold line per header cell, wrapped to the width.

**Width floor.** `renderTable` treats a `width` below 1 as 1, so it never throws on a degenerate width; through `renderMarkdown` the width is already floored by `resolveWidth`.

Use the already implemented `columnWidths` result to choose grid versus stacked, not a separate column-count heuristic. The grid remains the US-002 behavior when widths fit. Cells remain already-styled spans and header bold is added on top of their existing styles. A row with fewer cells than the header draws the missing cells empty; cells beyond the header count are not drawn. Keep `wrap.ts` read-only and call `wrap`, not the layout-floored `wrapSpans`.

**Scope** — In: extend `markdown-table.ts` and its existing tests with stacked body blocks, empty labels/values/rows, header-only output and the render width floor. Out: changing grid geometry or column shrinking (US-001/US-002), integrating markdown/table spies or changing reply-layout tests (US-004). Horizontal scrolling or a "show source" toggle for wide tables; the wrap-then-stack rule is the only answer to width. A configurable `MIN_CELL_WIDTH`, column-width hints, or a per-table style choice. Adding any package dependency for table rendering; the renderer is built on `marked`'s existing table token and `wrap.ts`. Exporting `markdown-table.ts` from the `src/tui/chat/index.ts` barrel; it has no consumer outside `src/tui/chat/`. `markdown-table.ts` importing `markdown.ts`; cells arrive as styled spans so the dependency runs one way only.

**Interface** — Preserve the upstream model/options definitions and this exported signature:
```ts
/** The grid when the columns fit, otherwise the stacked layout. */
export function renderTable(model: TableModel, options: RenderTableOptions): StyledLine[];
```
`RenderTableOptions` remains:
```ts
export interface RenderTableOptions {
  readonly width: number;
  readonly glyphs: TableGlyphs;
  /** The rule glyph the stacked layout separates rows with. */
  readonly rule: string;
}
```
Use `wrap(spans: readonly Span[], width: number, prefix?: readonly Span[]): StyledLine[]` for the combined label/value spans.

**Goal** — Route marked table tokens through the completed renderer and injected seam, preserving reply context, inline styles, sanitization and transcript gutters.

**Motivation** — `renderMarkdown` (`packages/nax/src/tui/chat/markdown.ts`) styles headings, lists, quotes, rules and fences, but its `blockLines` default branch returns `sourceLines(token.raw)` for a table, so a reply listing stories or a comparison shows raw `| a | b |` / `| - | - |` text.

**Approach** — One deterministic renderer, no new dependency. A new pure module `packages/nax/src/tui/chat/markdown-table.ts` owns column sizing and drawing over already-styled cell spans, so it does not import `markdown.ts` (no import cycle): `markdown.ts` turns each cell's inline tokens into spans with its existing `inlineSpans`, builds a `TableModel`, and calls `renderTable` through its `_markdownDeps` seam.

**Context.** The table renders at the width its block has, so inside a list item it renders behind the marker's indent and inside a blockquote behind the quote glyph, with the quote's italic carried into every cell through `baseStyle`. Cell text goes through `inlineSpans`, which sanitises it (`sanitizeText`), so a cell carrying `\u001b[?1049l` cannot leave the alternate screen. A half-streamed table whose delimiter row has not arrived yet is lexed by marked as a paragraph and keeps rendering as today; it becomes a grid on the delta that completes the delimiter row.

`packages/nax/src/tui/chat/markdown.ts` `_markdownDeps`. Target: `{ highlight: highlightToHtml, renderTable }`, with `renderTable` imported from `./markdown-table`.

`packages/nax/src/tui/chat/markdown.ts` `blockLines`. Target: `case "table"` builds a `TableModel` from the `Tokens.Table` (each header and row cell as `inlineSpans(cell.tokens, baseStyle(context))`, `align` copied) and returns `_markdownDeps.renderTable(model, { width: context.width, glyphs: context.theme.glyphs.table, rule: context.theme.glyphs.rule })`. The module header comment and the `default` branch comment stop listing tables among the source-text fallbacks.

`packages/nax/src/tui/chat/layout.ts` `replyLines`: calls `renderMarkdown(entry.text, { width: context.width - GUTTER_WIDTH, theme, minWidth: MIN_REPLY_WIDTH, cutCode: true })` and wraps any returned line still wider than the column in `prefixedLine`. Its behaviour is unchanged; a table line now never exceeds the width, so nothing reaches that wrap. Its doc comments (the module header and `replyLines`) and the `wrapSpans` doc comment in `wrap.ts` stop naming "a table's source text" as a wide line (US-004).

Test modifications: `packages/nax/test/unit/tui/chat/markdown.test.ts` — the test "keeps a table as its source text" asserts that `| a | b |\n| - | - |\n| 1 | 2 |` renders as its three source lines; a table now renders as a box grid, so that test is replaced by one asserting the grid lines `┌───┬───┐`, `│ a │ b │`, `├───┼───┤`, `│ 1 │ 2 │`, `└───┴───┘`. The raw HTML fallback test beside it stays unchanged. The spy test restores `_markdownDeps.renderTable` in an `afterEach` covering it (extend the existing hook that restores `_markdownDeps.highlight` in the fenced-code describe, or give the new describe its own), so the spy cannot leak into later tests.

`packages/nax/test/unit/tui/chat/layout.test.ts` — the test "wraps a table's source line instead of clipping it" asserts that the squashed layout of a 4-column header-only table equals its source text with whitespace removed; the table is now drawn by the renderer (stacked at this width), so the test instead asserts every line is at most `NARROW_WIDTH` columns and that the lines in order hold the header words `alpha`, `bravo`, `charlie`, `delta` and no `|` character.

**Scope** — In: `markdown.ts` dispatch/seam and comments; comment-only changes in `layout.ts` and `wrap.ts`; replace the obsolete table expectations and add styled-cell, sanitization, nested-block, streaming, ASCII and real `layoutTranscript` assertions in the existing markdown/layout tests. The renderer completed by US-003 is an input to this story. Out: renderer sizing/drawing changes (US-001–US-003), theme changes (US-001), layout runtime changes, nonreply markdown expansion, new dependencies and chat-barrel exports. Multi-line cell content from an inline `<br>` tag; it keeps rendering as the tag's text, as other raw HTML does. Tables in thinking blocks, user messages, tool results or listings; only replies go through `renderMarkdown`. Changing the fence glyphs (`codeTop`, `codeSide`, `codeBottom`) or sharing them with the table glyph set. Exporting `markdown-table.ts` from the `src/tui/chat/index.ts` barrel; it has no consumer outside `src/tui/chat/`. `markdown-table.ts` importing `markdown.ts`; cells arrive as styled spans so the dependency runs one way only. Raw HTML fallback behavior stays unchanged.

**Interface** — Preserve the completed renderer signature and model:
```ts
export type TableAlign = "left" | "center" | "right" | null;

export interface TableModel {
  readonly header: readonly (readonly Span[])[];
  readonly rows: readonly (readonly (readonly Span[])[])[];
  readonly align: readonly TableAlign[];
}

/** The grid when the columns fit, otherwise the stacked layout. */
export function renderTable(model: TableModel, options: RenderTableOptions): StyledLine[];
```
The seam target is:
```ts
export const _markdownDeps = {
  highlight: highlightToHtml,
  renderTable,
};
```
Use marked 18.1.0 `Tokens.Table`: `{ type: "table"; raw: string; align: Array<"center" | "left" | "right" | null>; header: TableCell[]; rows: TableCell[][] }`, `Tokens.TableCell`: `{ text: string; tokens: Token[]; header: boolean; align: ... }`. `renderMarkdown`'s existing public options and signature remain unchanged.
